import Colean.DataCmd

/-!
# Mutual blocks

```
mutual
codata Tree (α : Type u)
  | node (a : α) (children : Forest α)
codata Forest (α : Type u)
  | nil
  | cons (t : Tree α) (f : Forest α)
end
```

A `mutual … end` block whose declarations are all `codata`, or all `data`, with the same
parameters, is one family: the block `Tree_Forest` (the names joined by `_`) is declared as a
single family over the inductive `Tree_Forest.Ix`, which has a constructor per type, `Tree` and
`Forest`, whose arguments are the indices of that type, and every declaration of the single
type case is generated for the block (`Tree_Forest.F`, `Tree_Forest.corec`, `Tree_Forest.bisim`,
`Tree_Forest.rec`, …). This is how Lean itself elaborates mutual fixpoints, and the generic
theory needs nothing new. The types need not agree on their indices, since each has its own
constructor of `Ix`; where their universe is written, it must be the same. Then, per type:
`Tree α := Tree_Forest α .Tree`, and `Even n := Even_Odd (.Even n)` with an index, its
constructors `Tree.node` with their lemmas under `Tree`, `Tree.casesOn` with only the
constructors of `Tree`, registered as the `cases` eliminator, and aliases `Tree.corec`,
`Tree.bisim` (or `Tree.fold`) restricting the block-level declarations to the type. Dot notation
`t.dtor` resolves to `Tree_Forest.dtor` through the definition of `Tree`, and the lemmas are
stated with the block-level `dtor` and `ctor`. Corecursion and induction take a state or motive
over the whole block: `S : Tree_Forest.Ix α → Type _` and `c : ∀ i, S i → Tree_Forest.F α S i`
for `corec`, and `induction t using Tree_Forest.rec` for `data`, as with Lean's mutual
inductives, whose `induction` tactic does not apply to mutual types either.

Constructor names shared by several types of the block are qualified in the block-level
inductives (`Even_succ`, `Odd_succ` in `Even_Odd.F`); the per-type declarations keep the plain
names.

The block is analysed and generated by `Gen/` like a single declaration (`Gen/Front.lean`,
`Gen/Command.lean`); the per-type declarations are `Gen/Surface.lean`'s.

A `mutual` block mixing `data` and `codata` is rejected: such a block is a nested fixpoint, and
the nesting order is a choice. The `mixed_mutual` command that made it is removed, pending a
redesign.
-/

open Lean Elab Command

namespace Codata

@[command_elab Lean.Parser.Command.mutual] def elabMutualCodata : CommandElab := fun stx => do
  let cmds := stx[1].getArgs
  let decls := cmds.filterMap Gen.DeclStx.read?
  if decls.isEmpty then throwUnsupportedSyntax
  unless decls.size == cmds.size do
    throwError "a mutual block must consist of `codata` and `data` declarations only"
  if decls.size == 1 then
    elabCommand cmds[0]!
    return
  unless decls.all (·.co == decls[0]!.co) do
    throwError "a `mutual` block may not mix `data` and `codata` declarations"
  Gen.elabDecls decls stx

end Codata
